# ---------- 3 concentration ----------
b3 = """
<p>Headline numbers for <strong>tribal gaming revenue concentration</strong> tell two stories at once. Industry reporting puts tribal gaming's gross revenue at a record $46.2 billion in 2025, a figure that speaks to the sector's scale and its place in the American economy. The same reporting notes that about 56 percent of that revenue came from the top 9 percent of facilities. Both facts are true, and together they explain why debates over new revenue models, from mobile wagering to prediction-market partnerships, land so differently across Indian country.</p>
<p>This analysis looks at what concentration means in practice, why it persists, and what it implies for tribes with small or remote operations. It does not argue that concentration is a failure of the industry. It is largely a function of geography and law, and understanding it is a prerequisite for evaluating almost every policy proposal now under discussion.</p>
<h2>Why a few facilities earn so much</h2>
<p>Casino revenue follows population. A property within a short drive of a large metropolitan area competes for millions of potential customers, while one in a sparsely populated region draws on a fraction of that. This is the dynamic explored in our explainer on <a href="../why-location-decides-tribal-gaming-revenue-land-market-access-explainer/index.html">why location decides tribal gaming revenue</a>, and it helps explain the performance gap documented in our <a href="../urban-rural-tribal-casino-performance-gap-2026-analysis/index.html">urban-rural analysis</a>.</p>
<p>The Indian Gaming Regulatory Act reinforces the pattern. Gaming must take place on Indian lands, and a tribe's lands are determined by history, treaties, and federal land-into-trust decisions rather than by market opportunity. A nation whose reservation sits far from population centers cannot simply relocate. Some tribes have no gaming facility at all, a gap we examined in our report on <a href="../tribes-without-gaming-igra-access-gap-2026-analysis/index.html">tribes without gaming and the IGRA access gap</a>. The statute's off-reservation provisions exist but are narrow and slow, with governor concurrence and federal determinations as hurdles.</p>
<h2>What concentration does to the policy debate</h2>
<p>When revenue is concentrated, proposals are judged by who stands to gain. Sports betting under a compact, for example, tends to favor tribes with large existing customer bases, because a mobile or on-property sportsbook builds on loyalty programs and foot traffic. Our analysis of the <a href="../tribal-sportsbook-retention-engine-not-profit-center-2026-analysis/index.html">sportsbook as a retention engine</a> makes that point from the operator's side.</p>
<p>Prediction-market partnerships look different through the same lens. Tribal leaders who have signed them describe the revenue as a way to reach beyond a limited geography. Opponents, including the Indian Gaming Association, argue the legal risk is borne by exactly the smaller tribes least able to absorb it. Both positions respond to the same underlying fact: a tribe with a marginal casino has fewer options than one with a destination resort. Our coverage of the <a href="../igc-klasrobinson-study-prediction-markets-tribal-gaming-loss-scenarios-2026/index.html">loss scenarios for tribal gaming from prediction markets</a> models how the downside would fall unevenly.</p>
<h2>Reading the numbers carefully</h2>
<p>Two cautions apply. First, "facilities" and "tribes" are different units. A single nation may operate several properties, and the top 9 percent of facilities may be owned by a smaller share of tribes. Concentration by facility therefore does not translate directly into concentration by nation or by member. Second, gross revenue is not the same as what reaches members and governments. Operating expenses, debt service, regulatory costs, and reinvestment come first, and the use of net revenue is governed by IGRA's permitted purposes and each tribe's revenue allocation plan. For the mechanics, see our explainer on the <a href="../igra-net-revenue-allocation-five-permitted-uses-explainer/index.html">five permitted uses of net gaming revenue</a>.</p>
<p>Smaller operations also deliver value that revenue totals understate. A modest casino in a rural community may be the largest private employer in the county and a primary funder of tribal health, education and water services. The national figure is a poor guide to the importance of any single property to its community.</p>
<h2>Implications</h2>
<p>For policymakers, concentration argues for caution about proposals that assume a uniform tribal interest. For tribes, it underscores the value of diversification: non-gaming enterprises, hospitality investments, and shared-services arrangements can stabilize smaller economies. For regulators, it suggests that compliance burdens, including licensing and vendor requirements, weigh more heavily on operations with thin margins; our piece on <a href="../tribal-gaming-vendor-licensing-multi-jurisdiction-burden-2026-analysis/index.html">vendor licensing burdens</a> explores that. The broader numbers are collected in our <a href="../tribal-gaming-by-the-numbers-250-tribes-545-facilities-29-states-explainer/index.html">by-the-numbers explainer</a>, and the <a href="../../directory/index.html">tribal gaming directory</a> lets readers see the range of operations across states.</p>
<p>A record year for the industry and a skewed distribution of that record are not in tension. They are the same fact viewed from different distances, and any serious discussion of the sector's future has to hold both.</p>
"""
A.append(dict(slug="tribal-gaming-revenue-concentration-top-nine-percent-facilities-2026-analysis",
 title="Tribal Gaming Revenue Concentration: Why 9% of Casinos Earn 56%",
 description="Tribal gaming hit a record $46.2 billion in 2025, but revenue is highly concentrated. Here is what that means for smaller tribes and new revenue models.",
 dek="A record year for the industry and a lopsided distribution of that record are the same fact seen from different distances, and the policy debates now under way depend on both.",
 kicker="Economy · 6 min", section="economy", category_label="Economy", author="James Kingfisher",
 keywords="tribal gaming revenue concentration, tribal gaming revenue 2025, small tribes, tribal casinos",
 body=b3,
 links=[("../../directory/index.html","Tribal gaming directory"),("../../legal-guide/index.html","Legal Guide — IGRA & Class III")]))

# ---------- 4 Catawba SkyBoat ----------
b4 = """
<p>The <strong>sole proprietary interest</strong> requirement in the Indian Gaming Regulatory Act is one of the least-discussed provisions in the statute, yet it shapes how nearly every tribal casino is financed and developed. The Catawba Nation's experience with its Two Kings Casino in Kings Mountain, North Carolina, offers a concrete illustration. In 2022, the National Indian Gaming Commission found that the tribe's development contract with its former partner, SkyBoat Gaming, violated federal law because SkyBoat held too much control over the project. The tribe later settled with SkyBoat in May 2024, and the project moved forward with a different management arrangement.</p>
<p>This analysis uses that history to explain what the requirement means, how the NIGC evaluates contracts, and what tribes and their partners can learn. It is a discussion of publicly reported facts and general legal principles, not a characterization of any party's motives.</p>
<h2>What the requirement says</h2>
<p>IGRA declares that a principal purpose of the law is to ensure that the tribe is the primary beneficiary of its gaming operation. To that end, the statute requires that a tribe have the sole proprietary interest in, and responsibility for the conduct of, any gaming activity. Outside parties can help build and run a casino, but they cannot take ownership of it. Our <a href="../igra-management-contracts-explainer/index.html">explainer on IGRA management contracts</a> covers the approval process, fee limits and term limits that apply when a company manages an operation.</p>
<p>A recurring problem is that a contract that is labeled a development, consulting, or lease agreement can function as a management contract if it gives an outside party significant authority over gaming operations. Under the NIGC's framework, agreements that effectively confer management responsibility require the Commission's approval, and agreements that give outsiders ownership-like control can be void. Because a tribe borrowing to build a casino often relies on partners with capital, the line between assistance and control is where disputes arise.</p>
<h2>The Catawba sequence</h2>
<p>Public reporting describes the sequence as follows. The tribe's Kings Mountain project, planned as a $700 million permanent resort of roughly two million square feet with nearly 2,000 jobs, was initially developed with SkyBoat. In 2022 the NIGC concluded the development contract violated federal law because of the control SkyBoat held. In early May 2024, the tribe settled with SkyBoat, with terms not disclosed, removing the company from the project. Delaware North was named to develop and manage the resort, subject to NIGC approval of the contracts, and construction began that summer on a first phase with a later hotel and larger gaming floor.</p>
<p>Disputes with other parties followed, including litigation we have covered in our report on the <a href="../catawba-two-kings-kmld-developer-trespass-lawsuit-2026/index.html">Two Kings developer trespass lawsuit</a>. Readers can follow the project's progress through our coverage of the <a href="../catawba-two-kings-casino-phase-one-opens/index.html">phase one opening</a> and the <a href="../catawba-two-kings-permanent-resort-groundbreaking-2026/index.html">permanent resort groundbreaking</a>.</p>
<h2>Lessons for tribes and partners</h2>
<p>Several practical points emerge, none of them unique to Catawba. First, contract labels do not control; function does. A developer that sets operating policy, controls bank accounts, or can block major tribal decisions may be exercising management authority regardless of what the document is called. Second, early review matters. Submitting agreements to the NIGC for review before construction commits capital is far less costly than unwinding them afterward. Third, financing structures need the same scrutiny, since lenders and landlords sometimes seek protections that edge into control. Our analysis of <a href="../limited-sovereign-immunity-waivers-tribal-casino-contracts-lenders-vendors-2026/index.html">limited sovereign immunity waivers</a> and the discussion of <a href="../tribal-casino-lending-trust-land-collateral-explainer/index.html">trust land collateral</a> address adjacent issues.</p>
<p>Fourth, the economics of outside capital are changing. As investors, REITs and operators play larger roles in tribal projects, the number of agreements that sit near the line increases. Our piece on <a href="../reits-outside-capital-reshape-tribal-resort-financing-analysis-2026/index.html">outside capital and tribal resort financing</a> explores how that landscape is evolving, and the <a href="../../legal-guide/index.html">Legal Guide</a> offers a primer on the statutory framework.</p>
<h2>A fair reading</h2>
<p>It would be a mistake to treat the requirement as hostile to partnership. Tribes have always needed expertise, capital and operational know-how, and many management relationships have succeeded within the rules. The purpose of the provision is to keep the benefits and the authority with the tribe. When a contract is structured so that they remain there, the arrangement is likely to withstand review; when it is not, the consequences can include voided agreements, delays, and expensive settlements.</p>
<p>For anyone evaluating a tribal development, the question to ask is simple: who ultimately decides, and who ultimately benefits? The answer to both should be the tribe.</p>
"""
A.append(dict(slug="sole-proprietary-interest-igra-catawba-skyboat-nigc-finding-analysis",
 title="Sole Proprietary Interest Under IGRA: Lessons From Catawba and SkyBoat",
 description="IGRA requires tribes to hold the sole proprietary interest in gaming. The Catawba–SkyBoat history shows how the NIGC reviews development contracts.",
 dek="A 2022 NIGC finding and a 2024 settlement offer a public case study in where partnership ends and control begins in tribal casino development.",
 kicker="Sovereignty · 6 min", section="policy", category_label="Sovereignty", author="Sarah Begay",
 keywords="sole proprietary interest, IGRA management contract, NIGC, Catawba Two Kings",
 body=b4,
 links=[("../../legal-guide/index.html","Legal Guide — IGRA & Class III"),("../igra-management-contracts-explainer/index.html","IGRA management contracts explainer")]))

# ---------- 5 explainer: scope of gaming ----------
b5 = """
<p>How a state decides which games a tribe may offer is one of the most consequential and least intuitive questions in the Indian Gaming Regulatory Act. The rule is often summarized in a phrase: Class III gaming is allowed on Indian lands only if the state "permits such gaming for any purpose by any person, organization, or entity." This explainer on the <strong>scope of gaming under IGRA</strong> describes what that phrase means, how courts have read it, and why it shapes negotiations over everything from slot machines to sports betting.</p>
<h2>The statutory starting point</h2>
<p>IGRA divides tribal gaming into three classes. Class I covers traditional social games, Class II covers bingo and related games, and Class III covers everything else, including slot machines, banked card games, and pari-mutuel wagering. Our <a href="../class-ii-vs-class-iii-tribal-gaming-explainer/index.html">Class II versus Class III explainer</a> describes the distinctions. Class III gaming is lawful on Indian lands only if three conditions are met: it is authorized by a tribal ordinance approved by the National Indian Gaming Commission chair, it is located in a state that permits such gaming for any purpose by any person, organization, or entity, and it is conducted in conformance with a tribal-state compact.</p>
<p>The second condition is the one that defines scope. It ties what a tribe can offer to what the state allows anyone to offer, even in limited form.</p>
<h2>Two ways courts have read it</h2>
<p>The Supreme Court's decision in <em>California v. Cabazon Band of Mission Indians</em> (1987) distinguished between states that regulate gaming and states that prohibit it. If a state generally permits a type of gaming and merely regulates it, the Court reasoned, the state cannot enforce that regulation against a tribe on Indian lands; if the state prohibits the activity as a matter of public policy, it can. IGRA, passed the following year, built on that distinction. Our explainer on <a href="../california-v-cabazon-band-1987-tribal-gaming-explainer/index.html">Cabazon</a> and the <a href="../indian-gaming-regulatory-act-1988-history-cabazon-to-igra-explainer/index.html">history of IGRA</a> provide the background.</p>
<p>Applying the "any purpose, by any person" language, courts and the Interior Department have generally looked at whether a state allows a game in some form, such as charitable fundraising, a lottery, or a commercial license. If it does, the game can be subject to a compact. If a state criminally prohibits the game entirely, a tribe typically cannot negotiate for it. This is why a state's treatment of a single game can open or close a category.</p>
<h2>Why scope drives compact negotiations</h2>
<p>Scope sets the floor and the ceiling of what a compact can cover. A state that permits sports wagering in any form can be asked to negotiate over tribal sports betting, which is how many tribes added it in recent years. A state that restricts a game to one tribe through exclusivity gives that tribe a protected position, usually in return for revenue sharing; see our explainer on <a href="../exclusivity-clauses-tribal-compact-payment-suspension-explainer/index.html">exclusivity clauses and payment suspension</a>. Conversely, when a state authorizes a game for non-tribal operators, tribes with exclusivity may argue the bargain has changed, as in disputes over historical horse racing machines examined in our <a href="../historical-horse-racing-machines-tribal-exclusivity-flashpoint-analysis-2026/index.html">flashpoint analysis</a>.</p>
<p>The scope question also interacts with the good-faith duty. IGRA requires states to negotiate in good faith, but after <em>Seminole Tribe v. Florida</em> (1996), tribes generally cannot sue a state without its consent to enforce that duty, leading to the alternative procedures described in our explainers on <a href="../seminole-tribe-v-florida-1996-igra-compacting-explainer/index.html">that decision</a> and on <a href="../secretarial-procedures-class-iii-gaming-igra-explainer/index.html">secretarial procedures</a>.</p>
<h2>Common misconceptions</h2>
<p>One misconception is that a tribe may offer any game a state has ever allowed. In practice, the specific game, its legal category, and the state's current law all matter. Another is that scope is fixed. State legislatures can expand or contract what is permitted, and those changes can alter negotiating leverage. A third is that Class II games follow the same rules. They do not; Class II gaming is regulated primarily by tribes and the NIGC and does not require a compact, which is why Class II is significant in states that restrict Class III.</p>
<p>Finally, scope is not the only limit. Compacts may address licensing, regulatory responsibility, and the allocation of jurisdiction, but IGRA restricts states from imposing taxes on tribes, with payments permissible only in exchange for meaningful concessions. Our <a href="../tribal-state-compact-revenue-sharing-explainer/index.html">revenue-sharing explainer</a> addresses that boundary.</p>
<h2>Where to go next</h2>
<p>For a broader look at the statutory framework, the <a href="../../legal-guide/index.html">Legal Guide</a> collects primers on compacts, classes of gaming, and federal oversight. State-by-state context is available through the <a href="../../directory/index.html">tribal gaming directory</a>, which illustrates how differently each state has defined what is permitted. Understanding scope is the first step to reading any compact news with precision: nearly every headline about new games, exclusivity disputes, or expanded offerings turns on how this one phrase is applied.</p>
"""
A.append(dict(slug="scope-of-gaming-igra-any-purpose-by-any-person-class-iii-explainer",
 title="Scope of Gaming Under IGRA: What 'Any Purpose, by Any Person' Means",
 description="How IGRA's scope-of-gaming rule decides which Class III games a tribe can negotiate, from Cabazon to exclusivity and sports betting compacts.",
 dek="One statutory phrase links a state's gambling laws to what a tribe may offer, and it sits behind most compact headlines.",
 kicker="Explainer · 6 min", section="policy", category_label="Explainer", author="Daniel Yazzie",
 keywords="scope of gaming IGRA, Class III gaming, any purpose by any person, tribal-state compact",
 body=b5,
 links=[("../../legal-guide/index.html","Legal Guide — IGRA & Class III"),("../../directory/index.html","Tribal gaming directory")]))

